messages:
emily: Heads up — customer reported being charged twice for a single user action. They say our Node TS SDK retried the POST and the upstream accepted both. Repro steps below, looks tied to Node 20/undici timing and retries. 

alex: Can you paste the minimal repro and versions? Also, any logs or request ids? :mag:

emily: Repro + env: Node v20.8.0, undici 5.16.0, @redwood/js 2.3.1 (TS SDK). Using SDK's default retry/backoff. Example repro (simplified):

```js
// simplified client usage
const ac = new AbortController();
try {
  await client.post('/v1/generate', {
    body: JSON.stringify({prompt: 'hello'}),
    signal: ac.signal,
  });
} catch (e) {
  // SDK retries internally
}
// Note: customer reuses the same AbortController across attempts in their app
```

Logs from customer (timestamps redacted):
`req=abc123 -> upstream 200`
`req=abc123-retry1 -> upstream 200`

They see two successful upstream responses for the same logical request id.

jin: Thanks emily. A few quick Qs: are they streaming the response or using a single-body POST? Do they pass a non-rewindable stream (e.g., req stream) as body?

emily: Single-body JSON. Not streaming. The app creates one AbortController per high-level operation and keeps reusing it when SDK decides to retry (app-level code wraps call in its own retry loop).

jin: This rings like a timing/handshake edge in undici + our retry layer. If the original socket handshake is in-flight and undici aborts/reset, a configured retry may re-send the request, but if the body isn't buffer/cloneable the lower layer can end up re-sending bytes or duplicating attempts before the abort is fully observed. Also reusing the same AbortSignal across attempts can make it unclear whether the signal corresponds to the current attempt or a prior one.

Suggested immediate mitigations:
- Create a fresh AbortController per attempt (app or SDK).
- Buffer the request body (Buffer/Uint8Array) so it can be re-sent safely.
- Or add an idempotency header on the request `Idempotency-Key: <uuid>` so upstream will dedupe.

Patch snippet for user app (workaround):
```js
async function sendOnce(payload) {
  const body = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(payload)); // rewindable
  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    const ac = new AbortController(); // new per attempt
    try {
      return await client.post('/v1/generate', { body, signal: ac.signal });
    } catch (err) {
      // backoff then retry
    }
  }
}
```

sam: We rolled this workaround to the customer and they reported no duplicate charges after switching to a buffered body + new AbortController per try. They want an official SDK fix and a timeline. :thumbsup:

alex: Can eng triage? We should consider defensive behavior in the SDK: detect when body isn't cloneable and either refuse automatic retry, or buffer internally (configurable). Also avoid reusing caller-supplied AbortSignal across retries — we can create an internal per-attempt signal that ties to the caller signal.

eng-bot: Issue created: RDW-1189 — "Node SDK: handle non-rewindable bodies + per-attempt AbortController for retries". Assigned to @jin.

jin: Plan: 1) Add internal logic to wrap a user signal so we spawn a child controller per attempt that forwards cancel events but is distinct for attempt lifecycle. 2) Warn/log when body is a stream and auto-retry is enabled; opt to disable auto-retries for non-bufferable bodies by default. 3) Small patch to add an option `retry.rewindBodies: true|false|auto` and docs. I'll prepare a patch and test harness reproducer.

emily: Thanks. Customer asked for ETA — can we patch and ship a patch release?

jin: I'll target a patch in the next 48–72 hours. Will post a PR link and include a regression test that simulates undici handshake/drop timing.

alex: Great. Once PR is up, can we backport to the latest 2.x SDK? And add a note to the support KB with the workaround (buffer body / idempotency-key)?

sam: Adding KB note now. Thanks all.

emily: Closing the immediate support loop with the customer; escalating to eng for the SDK change.

alex: :white_check_mark:
